{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789820816,"nonce":"/G95E/dRbD13fIXj/wILWxkhrRo=","message":"Field-kind speculation for the Cranelift tier, examined and set aside: the class byte can flip after the compile (a native or interpreter store of a non-Num, or another thread), so a compiled loop that trusts it needs either a per-load byte check (no cheaper than the NaN-box check it replaces) or dependency tracking that deoptimises every body that assumed the kind when the byte changes. The LLVM tier gets away with an invariant load because LLVM hoists it, with the same hole under a mid-loop store. Not worth it without invalidation. Also tried: a read-only MemFlags on the fields-pointer load so Cranelift would merge the ten reloads per pair; the egraph did not merge them, no change. What the nbody inner loop pays now, from the disassembly: a fields-pointer reload and a NaN-box check per field access, a 7 KB frame with the loop counters spilled, and the inline subscript's eight-way type dispatch with an f64 spill on each path. The counters and the frame come from the deopt live sets keeping values alive across the whole loop; that is the lever if Cranelift stays the shipped tier.","files":null}]}